  "narration_beats": [
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_001",
      "attaches_to": "state",
      "target_id": "state_000",
      "text": "By the end of this video, you'll be able to open the Customers table in DB Browser for SQLite and see exactly how it stores fifty customer records.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 28,
      "start_time": 0.0,
      "end_time": 11.2,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_002",
      "attaches_to": "state",
      "target_id": "state_000",
      "text": "Think of a database table like a filing cabinet drawer: every folder inside is a row, and every folder has the same labeled tabs, which are the columns. In our Customers table, each folder is one customer, and the tabs are things like their name and signup date. This structure matters because it lets you find any customer's info in a predictable, consistent place.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 64,
      "start_time": 11.2,
      "end_time": 36.8,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_003",
      "attaches_to": "edge",
      "target_id": "edge_001",
      "text": "First, let's click the 'Browse Data' tab at the top of the window. This is the view built specifically for looking at table contents without writing any code, which is perfect for getting oriented.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 34,
      "start_time": 36.8,
      "end_time": 50.4,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_004",
      "attaches_to": "edge",
      "target_id": "edge_002",
      "text": "Now we need to tell the browser which table to open. There's a dropdown menu near the top of this panel listing every table in the database file.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 28,
      "start_time": 50.4,
      "end_time": 61.6,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_005",
      "attaches_to": "edge",
      "target_id": "edge_003",
      "text": "We'll select 'Customers' from that list. This loads the actual folder drawer we're interested in, the one holding every customer record in the business.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 24,
      "start_time": 61.6,
      "end_time": 71.2,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_006",
      "attaches_to": "edge",
      "target_id": "edge_004",
      "text": "Give it a second to load. You'll see the data fill in as a grid, rows running down and columns running across, exactly like folders lined up in a drawer with the same tabs on each one.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 37,
      "start_time": 71.2,
      "end_time": 86.0,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_007",
      "attaches_to": "state",
      "target_id": "state_004",
      "text": "Look at the bottom of the grid: it shows this table holds 50 rows, one for each customer, and across the top you'll count exactly 4 columns: customer_id, customer_name, country, and signup_date. That count is your proof the table loaded correctly.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 41,
      "start_time": 86.0,
      "end_time": 102.4,
      "audio_path": null
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "beat_008",
      "attaches_to": "state",
      "target_id": "state_004",
      "text": "You've just opened your first table and confirmed its shape: 50 customers, 4 pieces of information about each one. Next time, we'll open the Orders table and sort it by order_amount in ascending order, so you can see the smallest orders first.",
      "tts_text": null,
      "word_count": 42,
      "start_time": 102.4,
      "end_time": 119.2,
      "audio_path": null
    }
  ],
